.** Each input has a background of 0 with one large coloured rectangle on it. That rectangle is a patchwork of solid-colour blocks arranged on an irregular grid: each band of rows has its own height and each band of columns has its own width.\n\n**What the output is.** The output has exactly one cell per block. It shows the block layout in miniature, with the block sizes thrown away.\n\n- **Pair 1.** Rows 1–8 and 9–15 form two row bands. Columns 1–7 and 8–15 form two column bands. That gives a 2×2 output, `2 8 / 1 4`.\n- **Pair 3.** There are three row bands: 2–6, 7–12 and 13–18. There are two column bands, split at x=8. The middle row band is entirely 3, so the output is 3×2 with `3 3` in the middle row. This shows that a block spanning several cells is simply repeated in each cell it covers.\n- **Pair 2.** The column cuts come from all rows combined. Rows 7–10 change colour at x=9 and again at x=15. Every other row band changes only at x=9. Cutting the columns at every change point gives three column bands: 1–8, 9–14 and 15–20. So the output is 3×3, and the wide 7 and 5 blocks each fill two output cells: `8 7 7 / 3 4 1 / 2 5 5`.\n\n**The rule:**\n1. Crop to the bounding box of the non-background cells.\n2. Start a new row band at every row that differs from the row above it.\n3. Start a new column band at every column that differs from the column to its left.\n4. For each pair of row band and column band, output the colour at the band's first cell.\n\nI applied these steps by hand to the band boundaries listed above, and they reproduce all three training outputs. The program itself is checked by the harness.\n\n**Test input.**\n- Row bands: 1–6, 7–14 and 15–18.\n- Column bands: 1–5, 6–13 and 14–18.\n\nThe expected output is therefore:\n\n\n\n**Teaching stages emitted by the program:**\n1. The cropped patchwork.\n2. The empty miniature grid, with its size fixed by the band counts.\n3. The miniature filled in one row band at a time.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    ys, xs = np.where(g != bg)\n    c = g[ys.min():ys.max() + 1, xs.min():xs.max() + 1].copy()\n    emit(c, \"Crop to the coloured patchwork of rectangular blocks.\")\n    H, W = c.shape\n    rstarts = [0] + [y for y in range(1, H) if not np.array_equal(c[y], c[y - 1])]\n    cstarts = [0] + [x for x in range(1, W) if not np.array_equal(c[:, x], c[:, x - 1])]\n    out = np.full((len(rstarts), len(cstarts)), c[0, 0], dtype=int)\n    out[:] = 0\n    emit(out.copy(), f\"Rows change {len(rstarts)} times and columns {len(cstarts)} times: allocate a {len(rstarts)}x{len(cstarts)} grid, one cell per block.\")\n    for i, y in enumerate(rstarts):\n        for j, x in enumerate(cstarts):\n            out[i, j] = c[y, x]\n        emit(out.copy(), f\"Fill row band {i} with the colour of each block in that band.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The empty miniature grid, with its size fixed by the band counts."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Crop to the bounding box of the non-background cells."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The cropped patchwork."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Crop to the coloured patchwork of rectangular blocks."}],"scale":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"**What the output is.** The output has exactly one cell per block."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It shows the block layout in miniature, with the block sizes thrown away."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The empty miniature grid, with its size fixed by the band counts."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"91d0863885adcd7820447726a63bb8d29e09bc6439f768c299da2af40d88e5b8","task_sha256":"66af0fc0922ee5e7b8e6d180f94c9c00f5b4b8db7b353f1c2673c336a9860f55","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
